      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The feeling of resistance or sabotage is a common phenomenon reported by athletes experiencing plateaus. From a biological perspective, this can be attributed to the way the nervous system processes and interprets sensory feedback during exercise.\n\nWhen an individual is performing a movement like back squats, their muscles, joints, and nervous system are constantly sending and receiving signals. These signals can be thought of as a form of \"feedback\" that helps the nervous system adjust and refine the movement. However, when the CNS is fatigued, this feedback loop can become disrupted.\n\nOne key player in this feedback loop is the proprioceptive system, which is responsible for detecting changes in joint position, movement, and muscle tension. When the proprioceptive system is fatigued, it can lead to an overestimation of the load or resistance being applied to the joints. This can create a sense of \"malicious resistance\" or \"sabotage\" that feels like the weight is actively fighting the individual.\n\nAdditionally, the brain's motor control systems, particularly the cerebellum and basal ganglia, play a crucial role in processing and refining movement patterns. When these systems are fatigued, they can lead to an increase in \"error\" signals, which can manifest as",
